  <h1>What to Do If Your Employer Won't Pay Overtime</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Sam Calloway (SC), Contributing Writer &mdash; Settlement and Practice. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>If your employer isn't paying overtime you're owed, you have real options &mdash; and most of them are free or low-cost. The federal Fair Labor Standards Act and almost every state's wage law provide multiple paths to recovery, including agency complaints that don't require an attorney and private litigation that's typically pursued on contingency. This guide is a sequenced playbook: what to do in the first week, what to do in the first month, and how to decide whether to pursue an agency claim, a lawsuit, or both.</p>
  <p>It's informational. It is not legal advice and reading it does not create an attorney-client relationship.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: Document everything</h2>
  <p>Start a personal record of your hours immediately. Don't wait. The more contemporaneous your records, the more weight they carry with an agency investigator or in court. Keep:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Daily start and end times. If your employer uses a time clock, save copies of your weekly timecards.</li>
    <li>Meal break length (and whether you actually got one). Brief breaks where you were "available" for work generally don't count as a bona fide meal break.</li>
    <li>Off-the-clock work: emails answered after hours, calls taken on the way home, pre-shift prep, post-shift cleanup, on-call time meeting the test for compensable on-call.</li>
    <li>Every pay stub. Save digital copies in a personal account separate from employer systems.</li>
    <li>Communications about your hours: emails, texts, schedules, manager comments about staying late or coming in early.</li>
    <li>Job descriptions, offer letters, and any "exempt status" documentation you have.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do this BEFORE you raise the issue. If documentation only surfaces after a complaint, employers can argue you fabricated records to support the claim. Contemporaneous records carry far more weight.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: Calculate what you're owed</h2>
  <p>Use our <a href="/">overtime calculator</a> to estimate. Be conservative &mdash; calculate based on hours you can prove with documentation. Go through your records and look at every workweek where you worked over 40 hours (or over your state's daily threshold if you're in California, Alaska, Nevada, or Colorado).</p>
  <p>Don't forget to include non-discretionary bonuses, commissions, and shift differentials in the regular rate (per our <a href="/guides/how-overtime-is-calculated">overtime calculation guide</a>). Underpayment often comes from the employer using base wage as the overtime base instead of the regular rate inclusive of these supplemental payments.</p>
  <p><strong>Statute of limitations.</strong> Federal FLSA: 2 years for non-willful violations, 3 years for willful. State limits vary; California is 3 years, New York is 6 years, Texas is 2 years for most claims. Don't sit on a claim &mdash; once the statute runs, you're done regardless of how strong the underlying case is.</p>

  <h2>Step 3: Try internal resolution (if it's safe)</h2>
  <p>Sometimes underpayment is a payroll error that can be fixed with a polite written request. A clear email to HR or payroll, with your documented hours and the specific weeks you believe were miscalculated, sometimes resolves it. Get any response in writing.</p>
  <p>Don't do this if you fear retaliation. While retaliation for raising a wage claim is illegal under FLSA &sect; 15(a)(3) and every state law, prevention is better than enforcement. If you reasonably believe raising the issue will result in adverse employment action and you don't yet have other documentation of your performance, the smarter sequence is documentation first, complaint to an enforcement agency second, internal resolution rarely.</p>
  <p>If you do raise the issue internally, frame it as a question or potential error, not as an accusation &mdash; "I noticed my paystub for the week of X shows OT calculated on $X but my bonus was paid out separately and I think it should have been included; can someone confirm?" is more productive than "you've been underpaying me." That said, the legal protection is the same either way; retaliation for any good-faith wage complaint is illegal.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: File a wage claim (free)</h2>
  <p>You have two main options, and you can use both:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>US Department of Labor Wage and Hour Division (WHD)</strong> at dol.gov/whd handles federal FLSA claims. Filing is free. You don't need an attorney. WHD investigates, often interviews coworkers and reviews payroll records, and can recover back wages plus liquidated damages (an amount equal to back wages, doubling the recovery). WHD investigations can take several months to a year.</li>
    <li><strong>Your state's labor agency</strong> handles state-law claims. California's DLSE, New York DOL, Massachusetts AGO Fair Labor Division, Texas Workforce Commission, etc. State recovery is sometimes faster than federal for amounts within state limits, and state laws often allow recovery for items federal law doesn't (waiting-time penalties under California, spread-of-hours pay under New York).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You don't have to choose. Many workers file with both. The only constraint is that you can't recover the same back wages twice &mdash; if a state agency awards you $5,000 in back wages and a federal investigation also identifies $5,000, you take one payment, not both.</p>
  <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Agency claims have built-in privacy protections in most cases. WHD does not disclose the identity of the complaining worker to the employer during the initial investigation if you ask for confidentiality, although the employer often can infer who complained. State agencies vary in their confidentiality protections; ask before filing.</p>

  <h2>Step 5: Consider an attorney for larger amounts</h2>
  <p>For unpaid overtime over a few thousand dollars, a wage-and-hour attorney can often recover more than agency claims. Reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Federal FLSA allows recovery of back wages, an equal amount in liquidated damages, plus reasonable attorney's fees and costs. Most state wage laws have similar fee-shifting provisions.</li>
    <li>Private suits can pursue collective or class actions when multiple workers are affected by the same misclassification or pay practice. Class actions are often the only practical route for systemic violations.</li>
    <li>Attorneys typically work on contingency for wage-and-hour cases &mdash; no fee unless you recover &mdash; because the fee-shifting provisions guarantee payment from the employer on success.</li>
    <li>Discovery in a lawsuit can surface payroll records and internal communications that an agency investigation may not reach.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Most wage-and-hour attorneys offer free initial consultations. Even one call can tell you whether your case is worth pursuing privately and approximate what the recovery range looks like.</p>

  <h2>What if I'm afraid of retaliation?</h2>
  <p>Retaliation for wage complaints is illegal under federal FLSA &sect; 15(a)(3) and every state's wage law. If you are fired, demoted, reassigned, or punished after raising a wage issue or filing a complaint, that's a separate violation with its own remedies &mdash; often including reinstatement, back pay, and additional damages on top of any underlying wage recovery.</p>
  <p>The practical reality is that retaliation is hard to prove if your performance was already a documented concern, and easier to prove when the timing is suspicious. Strengthen the timing argument by:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Keeping copies of any positive performance reviews, attendance records, and customer feedback from before you complain.</li>
    <li>Filing the complaint in writing (email or formal letter) so the date is established.</li>
    <li>Documenting any change in how you're treated after the complaint &mdash; changes in schedule, work assignments, manager interactions, performance reviews.</li>
    <li>If you're fired, keeping records of the stated reason and any inconsistencies in how that reason has been applied to other workers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common settlement outcomes</h2>
  <p>Most wage-and-hour claims don't go to trial. They settle &mdash; either during an agency investigation, after a demand letter from an attorney, or in private mediation early in litigation. Typical settlement levels:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Agency-investigated cases: back wages plus liquidated damages where willful, sometimes negotiated down depending on the employer's cooperation.</li>
    <li>Private individual cases: back wages, liquidated damages, attorney's fees, possibly an additional negotiated amount for the inconvenience and uncertainty.</li>
    <li>Class or collective actions: complex; recovery per worker can be smaller individually but more workers are reached. Settlements often include systemic relief (changes to the employer's pay practices going forward).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What an employer's defenses typically look like</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>"You were exempt." Most common &mdash; especially "manager" or "professional" misclassification. Counter with the duties test: what did you actually do day-to-day, and was your primary duty exempt work or non-exempt work?</li>
    <li>"You didn't actually work those hours." Counter with your contemporaneous records and any corroborating evidence (emails, communications, coworker testimony).</li>
    <li>"You agreed to a comp time arrangement." For private-sector employees, FLSA does not allow private employers to substitute comp time for overtime pay. The agreement is generally unenforceable.</li>
    <li>"The statute of limitations has run." Counter by demonstrating willfulness (which extends federal to 3 years) and by checking whether your state has a longer statute.</li>
    <li>"You signed a waiver." FLSA rights cannot be waived by private agreement. A waiver of overtime entitlement is unenforceable.</li>
  </ul>

